A||||3||montage||coolant pipe (heater)||||1||
Z||000000||montage||12-14-2000||02:51 PM||||I recently purchased my first Fiero, a 1988 5-speed Formula, and must say I've never driven a car that was so much fun. However, I discovered a leak on what looks like a black metallic heater coolant line that goes under the car. What are the options for repair and are replacements available? Secondly, what materials are these made from (Al)? My thoughts on repair include liquid solder or JB weld, or cutting the tube and splicing in some rubber heater hose. Additional, I'd like to thank all the members of the forum for all the great information I've been reading. ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Old Lar||12-14-2000||09:41 PM||||The coolant tubes that run under the car are made from a stainless steel that will corrode under the clamps that mount them to the frame.  Enough corrosion, they leak.  (a common occurance) I'm sure there are repairs to fix the holes. I don't know of any. If they are black, someone has painted the underside of your car. Rodney Dickman sells replacement coolant tubes made from much better stuff.<P>Rodney can be found on the web ring or do a search on the forum to locate his web page.||||reg||3
Z||000002||PlasticWrapd||12-14-2000||09:55 PM||||Hmmm.. depends on where the line is and the size.  The large pipes running down the outer (close to the doors) part of the car are coolant lines going to the radiator. The smaller ones, closer to the center, are heater lines. The most likely cause is one of the connecting hoses is leaking and the coolant is running down to the lowest part of the pipe where it drips off.  Check the hoses before you panic  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Raydar||12-15-2000||02:47 PM||||The worst problem area for leaks on the 88 V-6 is the coolant crossover pipe. It is a painted steel pipe, routed from the thermostat housing hose, between the A/C compressor and the block, under the front manifold, almost to the left side of the car. Another hose connects it to the stainless pipe, under the driver's side door. Many of us have had to replace it on our cars. *Do* check for loose clamps, first.<BR>Old Lar mentioned Rodney Dickman's stainless replacement pipe. I also recommend him highly.  <A HREF="http://www.rodneydickman.com/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.rodneydickman.com/index.html</A> <BR>Good luck.<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 12-15-2000).]||||reg||1
